# pots v1.4 — NEGATIVE. A heart rate above the shape's physiological ceiling.
#
# 350 bpm is not a plausible human heart rate at any age, and that is the point:
# pots:HeartRateMeasurementShape binds the value to 20-300 bpm at sh:Violation,
# and this is the reading a sensor glitch or a unit mix-up produces. A device
# that reports in a different unit, or an artefact during motion, is how a number
# like this reaches a pod, and a record carrying it should be rejected rather
# than filed as a real observation.
#
# EXACTLY ONE CONSTRAINT FIRES, and the runner prints it like this:
#   pots:HeartRateMeasurementShape / path _:blank / MaxInclusiveConstraintComponent
#     :: "Heart rate value must be 20-300 bpm"
#
# THE `_:blank` IS NOT A FAULT IN THE FIXTURE. The constraint's sh:path is the
# SEQUENCE PATH ( fhir:valueQuantity fhir:value ), which SHACL encodes as an RDF
# list -- a blank node -- and the runner's path renderer prints a blank node
# rather than expanding the list. Every sequence-path constraint in the
# specification reports this way. The sh:message is what identifies it, which is
# why the message is quoted above. Do not go looking for a property literally
# named _:blank, and do not "fix" the fixture to make the name appear.
#
# Everything else is correct and identical to heartrate-standing.VALID.ttl apart
# from the subject IRI and the value: fhir:valueQuantity is present so the
# sh:minCount 1 above it is satisfied, the datatype is xsd:double so
# sh:datatype passes, and BOTH timing properties are present, Info-severity and
# in range. The one thing wrong is the one thing that fails, which is what makes
# this a test of the range constraint rather than of the record as a whole.
#
# THE PARITY IS EXACT AND IS MAINTAINED DELIBERATELY. pots:HeartRateMeasurement-
# Shape declares two Info-severity timing constraints, on pots:secondsSinceStand
# and on pots:atSeconds (which pots.ttl:297 calls the legacy spelling), and both
# files carry both properties with the same values. A reader comparing the two
# graphs should find exactly two differences, the subject and the value, and the
# claim above is only worth making if that stays true -- so a property added to
# either file belongs in the other.
#
# 350 rather than 301 deliberately. A boundary value tests the inclusivity of the
# bound, which is worth doing but is a different assertion, and a fixture sitting
# one bpm outside is fragile against a future decision to widen the range for
# infant data. This value is wrong under any plausible bound.
#
# Fully synthetic.

@prefix pots: <https://ns.cascadeprotocol.org/pots/v1#> .
@prefix fhir: <http://hl7.org/fhir/> .
@prefix loinc: <http://loinc.org/rdf#> .
@prefix ucum: <http://unitsofmeasure.org/> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<urn:uuid:b0750003-0000-4a00-8000-000000001020> a pots:HeartRateMeasurement ;
    fhir:code loinc:8867-4 ;
    fhir:valueQuantity [
        fhir:value "350.0"^^xsd:double ;
        fhir:unit "beats/minute" ;
        fhir:code ucum:min-1
    ] ;
    pots:secondsSinceStand "60.0"^^xsd:double ;
    pots:atSeconds "60"^^xsd:integer .
